  },
  {
    "episode_id": 75416,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75416.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "This is a naive answer in a way, because I look forward to the day that you don't look at me, say, I'm a white girl. I don't look at you and say you're a black man. I look forward to the day that we're judging each other and who we are inside that value. We have a lot of challenges still ahead. That is, I can't sugarcoat it this summer. participated in many of the Black Lives Matter Walks rallies, probably close to 25 of them, talked to a lot of people, seen a lot of anger, and seen a lot of hurt. But I think COVID is an opportunity to shine a light again on disparities and some of the structural issues that we have have. We desperately need criminal justice reform. I could go to the list here. But what I'm hopeful is that maybe by 2042, we'll have had some honest and comfortable conversations. that we will keep moving the ball forward that will stop identity politics and really just locate each other as just being a great person or if you're someone that supports Michigan State, I'm gonna like, you know, how could you not support Michigan? But that was not like this. I am hopeful by 2042 we're not as conscious of grace, class, and status as we are now. It'd be idealistic, but it's where I want to get.",
