    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75416.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "I can't believe it's already almost February 2021. I'm still kind of like trying to come to grips with what exactly happened last year. You know, it was especially weird for us political reporters because You know, the coronavirus kind of happened the same night as the Democratic primary. We were all in the office. You know, the word had come down that you go by and did one Michigan pretty handily there against Bernie Sanders, that primary. And then like two hours later, Governor Whitmer is holding a press conference about the first case of COVID in our state. And it was like immediately, you know, everything just shifted to that. We all kind of became public health supporters. You guys too, everybody started covering this pandemic, and that was obviously top of mind. And it really changed the dynamic of the election. There were a good two months or so, give or take. It's hard to remember exactly where there was no real campaigning that could happen. you know we weren't sure how public events could take place we weren't sure I mean obviously both of the candidates are guys in their 70s and we're at that you know at risk level so we didn't really know what it was going to look like you know we saw kind of two totally different versions of a presidential campaign where you know the Biden camp was very restrained in what they were doing you know they would have a dozen people or so at events in a parking lot outside separated, you know, in little circles. I'm sure everybody's seen the pictures of what those events have looked like by now.",
