  {
    "episode_id": 75416,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75416.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 49,
    "turn_text": "Well, I mean, first of all, I think he is going to have a hard time in the first months of this year bringing the country back together. And that's, you know, he he kind of outlined some of those problems in his inauguration speech that we're already dealing with, you know, all of these issues, you know, the the fight for criminal justice reform, huge unemployment issues and economic devastation from the pandemic, the health crisis that comes with that, the deaths and the sicknesses that we're still dealing with as we get this vaccine rolling out. And this just unprecedented, I guess, certainly in our lifetimes, polarization and division. I think in terms of Michigan, you guys talked with Congressman Dingell. He's got really strong relationships to people like her. He's really been close friends with Debbie Stabenow, our senior senator. He worked with her in Congress for a number of years. Your strong relationship with our governor over the last, you know, year and a half or so as he's been campaigning here and working with her. He's got strong relationships with the mayor of Detroit. You know, he helps Mayor Duggan helps run his campaign. His son got involved in that too. And, you know, all across the state. I mean, he's got strong relationships with folks here in Michigan dating back to when he was VP, you know, coming in to help Detroit with their bankruptcy and the auto bailout and all that stuff. So I think that's going to create a unique opportunity that we certainly saw a bit of that in the Trump administration with, you know, folks like Betsy DeVos and Ben Carson. But this is a little bit different. I think it's going to create more opportunities for investment to come into some of our underserved communities. Biden's talked a lot about creating urban agenda that has people in Detroit pretty excited.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The new leader will face significant challenges in reuniting the country during the first months of the year.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Biden has strong personal relationships with key figures in Michigan, including Congressman Dingell, Senator Debbie Stabenow, the state governor, and the mayor of Detroit.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The inauguration speech highlighted existing national problems including criminal justice reform, unemployment, economic devastation from the pandemic, ongoing health crises, and extreme polarization.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Biden previously helped manage Detroit’s bankruptcy and supported the auto industry bailout while serving as Vice President.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a high level of political polarization in the United States that is unprecedented in living memory.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
