    "turn_text": "think that actually has that idea that whites will no longer be in the majority is driving a lot of the strife that we're seeing right now in the country. Especially the folks that seem to be going down these kind of rabbit holes about the election, about, you know, COVID-19, this whole kind of conspiracy alternate reality that we have you know, percolating in the background. You know, a lot of it is driven by this kind of feeling of threat that those people have. So I think you guys are, you know, on something, this is going to be kind of a big. cultural kind of change that we're going to go through it. Not that it's a bad thing, but just that, you know, we're already seeing the effect of people, you know, dealing with that. But it really feels like we're at kind of an inflection point right now, right? I mean, it could really go one of two ways. And I think that, you know, we're going to see in the next 22 years, 21 years, I guess, or at least I'm hopeful that we'll see some action toward some of these kind of social issues that we've seen. There's been certainly a lot of pushback on the civil rights calls for justice and things like that, police reform. But Generally, I think things are moving in that direction. We're going to see progress toward that. It's going to be slow and it's going to be messy. But I'm hopeful that there seems to be will to fix some of those systemic issues and not just in policing, but systemic issues in education and opportunity of outcome and all that stuff. I think that's going to probably get better. And especially as people of color, people who have been marginalized and not given positions of power are finding more access to that now. Our government is looking more diverse. Our institutions are looking more diverse, not as much as they probably should be for this point in history. We get a long way to go on that stuff still, but I think it's all headed in the right direction. You know, what I'm more worried about is this kind of new resurgence in political extremism. You know, what happened to the Capitol is I just didn't ever expect us to get to a point where we would see people to the halls of cups to stop. Yeah.",
